WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.540
A slipper snail embryo makes its mouth from the first opening of its gut.

2
00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.620
Its anus appears about 12 days after fertilization, from a different set of cells.

3
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.500
Facts like this have long been used to sort animals into two great groups.

4
00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.360
Does that sorting work?

5
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:27.713
The first opening of the embryonic gut is called the blastopore.

6
00:00:28.453 --> 00:00:33.773
In 1908, Karl Grobben proposed splitting the bilaterian animals by its fate.

7
00:00:34.673 --> 00:00:39.273
In protostomes, meaning mouth first, the blastopore becomes the mouth.

8
00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:44.113
In deuterostomes, meaning mouth second, the mouth forms separately.

9
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:46.673
The blastopore often becomes the anus.

10
00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:55.647
In 2015, Lyons, Perry and Henry checked the rule in the slipper snail, Crepidula

11
00:00:55.647 --> 00:01:01.607
fornicata. They injected fluorescent tracers into chosen cells, and followed the

12
00:01:01.607 --> 00:01:07.367
descendants. The blastopore became the mouth, and the anus formed late, from other

13
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:11.487
cells. So this snail is protostomic by fate map.

14
00:01:12.107 --> 00:01:16.447
The study does not tell us about the snail's wider group, the spiralians.

15
00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:20.907
And blastopore fate is not a reliable test of the major clades.

16
00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:29.357
In 1951, volumes 2 and 3 of Libbie Hyman's The Invertebrates ordered the bilaterians

17
00:01:29.357 --> 00:01:36.357
by cavity. Acoelomates have tissue filling the space between gut and body wall, as

18
00:01:36.357 --> 00:01:37.697
in a planarian flatworm.

19
00:01:38.977 --> 00:01:44.297
Pseudocoelomates have a fluid-filled cavity without a complete mesoderm lining, as

20
00:01:44.297 --> 00:01:46.117
in the nematode C. elegans.

21
00:01:47.297 --> 00:01:51.937
Coelomates have a cavity lined entirely by mesoderm, as in the earthworm.

22
00:01:53.237 --> 00:01:57.117
Animals that share a level of organization like this form a grade.

23
00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.700
A clade is different.

24
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.040
It is an ancestor and all of its descendants.

25
00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:11.780
In 1997, Aguinaldo, Lake and colleagues compared the 18S ribosomal RNA gene across

26
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.760
animals. Nematodes grouped with arthropods, not with other worms.

27
00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:21.360
Both moult a cuticle, so the group is called Ecdysozoa.

28
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.380
Later analyses of many genes broadly support it.

29
00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.460
Flatworms sit inside Spiralia, with annelids and molluscs.

30
00:02:31.233 --> 00:02:35.873
So body cavity describes a grade, not a clade.

31
00:02:36.513 --> 00:02:42.493
A flatworm's acoelomate body may well be a secondary simplification, though the

32
00:02:42.493 --> 00:02:44.293
ancestral state is still debated.

33
00:02:45.553 --> 00:02:49.833
To test a grouping, sample hundreds of genes from many species.

34
00:02:50.533 --> 00:02:54.793
Then add independent evidence, such as which genes share chromosomes.

35
00:02:56.153 --> 00:02:58.573
Body-plan characters describe animals.

36
00:02:58.573 --> 00:03:01.673
Independent evidence tests their ancestry.
